<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oda Nobukatsu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1558">1558</a>–<a href="/years/1630">1630</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oda Nobukatsu was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. In 1579 he invaded Iga province without his father's permission and was defeated, an affront that Nobunaga avenged in 1581. After Nobunaga's death he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> in 1584 but later submitted, and under the Tokugawa he ended his life as a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobukatsu.jpg" alt="Oda Nobukatsu" width="320" height="596" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 投稿者がファイル作成 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oda_Nobukatu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In 1570 Nobukatsu was made the adopted heir of the Kitabatake clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-province">Ise Province</a> and married a daughter of its former lord, Kitabatake Tomonori, a match that was in truth a condition of the truce the Oda forced on the Kitabatake. For a time he bore the name Kitabatake Tomotoyo. He became head of the family officially in 1575, and the following year he moved against its former leaders by killing and imprisoning them and took over the clan completely.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first invasion of Iga ended in disastrous failure and a severe rebuke from his father. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and his heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobutada">Oda Nobutada</a> died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honnō-ji</a> incident of 1582, Nobukatsu and his younger brother Oda Nobutaka quarrelled over the succession, and a council, swayed above all by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, chose Nobutada's infant son Oda Hidenobu. Nobukatsu took back the surname Oda and succeeded his father as lord of Owari, Mino and Ise.</p><p>In 1583 he joined Hideyoshi to destroy Nobutaka, but the two soon fell out. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-komaki-and-nagakute">Battle of Komaki and Nagakute</a> he killed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> named Okada Sukesaburō with a 13th-century tachi of the Fukuoka Ichimonji school, afterwards called Okada-giri Yoshifusa and now a national treasure. After more than half a year of fighting, Hideyoshi persuaded him to make peace in return for keeping his lands, and he became in practice Hideyoshi's retainer.</p><p>During the Odawara Campaign of 1590 he refused Hideyoshi's order to move to another domain, lost his lands and was made to become a monk under the watch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> retainers. Some years later he regained a little land, and in 1598, after Hideyoshi's death, he became a guardian of Toyotomi Hideyori.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-osaka">Siege of Osaka</a> in 1615 he abandoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> and surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> let him remain a daimyō as lord of the Uda-Matsuyama Domain in Yamato Province. He died on 10 June 1630. Though often described as an incompetent general, he survived the upheavals that ended his family's power, and his daughter O-hime was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Tokugawa Hidetada</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oda Nobukatsu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1579 Oda Nobunaga's son <b>Nobukatsu</b> invaded Iga and was beaten back.</blockquote><a href="/state/iga-confederacy">Iga confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
